    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_fbf1d38ce4963640361ffa78e784a0224c42103a96816f6a5cd855062997a3d3",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Prime-square averages carry two related but distinct coarse signals. In quadratic-character value bounds, their coherent contribution shifts the center before the remaining prime polynomial fluctuates; in family trace statistics, the prime-square average is the Frobenius-Schur indicator distinguishing orthogonal from symplectic type. Center shifts, symmetry type, functional-equation sign, and density observability should therefore be compared without identifying them.",
